body:
@annevk:
Because we allow ALL `web+...:` schemes, I conclude that the argument is not about having to much schemes allowed. The real discussion is safety. Somehow, all `web+...:` are safe, likely because they aren't used normally by other applications. We should think about what can harm people if we allow all schemes. I see many schemes that aren't allowed at the moment, that can really help make the web even more useful, like `market:`. I think you should come up with a real example what could go wrong if we allow it all. What are clearly dangerous schemes, why, and how? Also, take in mind that UX things can be done too, like above. Maybe as developer, I want to destroy my whole system by letting by browser handle a dark scheme and want to have a way of allowing that scheme regardless of the current policy. I also think browsers should move that list to a config variable and not immutably hardcode it into the browser.

@nektro:
> i think the arguments for the blocklist are the best

Okay, maybe a blocklist for really dangerous schemes can be made, like `chrome:`, `about:`, `http:` and `https:`. Sure. But for others, I still think a graylist is fine.

@domenic:
> The browsers aren't going to ship something just because some list maintained elsewhere (e.g. IANA) said they should;

Well, I don't think browsers should just implement a IANA registry. I think the WHATWG spec should say that browser should implement the IANA registry. This makes it likely easier to maintain a list and it makes it also possible to use process of registration that is different from the one that is used to contribute to the WHATWG spec.
